{0 Workflows}

A {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/}Workflow} is a durable,
multi-step program. Each step runs at least once, its result is stored, and the
runtime can sleep, retry and resume the program for days. {!Workero.Workflow}
covers both halves: the class that runs the steps, and the binding that starts
and controls instances.

{1 Write a Workflow}

Register the class under a name at module initialisation. The function gets the
[env], the event and the step object:

{@ocaml[
open Workero
module Step = Workflow.Step

let () =
  Workflow.register "Greet" (fun _env event step ->
      let name =
        Step.do_ step "read name" (fun _ ->
            match event.Workflow.payload with
            | `Assoc [ ("name", `String n) ] -> `String n
            | _ -> `String "world")
      in
      Step.sleep step "pause" (Workflow.Duration.seconds 10);
      let greeting =
        Step.do_ step "greet"
          ~retries:
            (Step.retries ~limit:3 ~delay:(Workflow.Duration.seconds 5)
               ~backoff:Step.Exponential ())
          ~timeout:(Workflow.Duration.minutes 1)
          (fun { Step.attempt; _ } ->
            let res = fetch "https://example.com/greeting" in
            Console.log (Printf.sprintf "greeting, attempt %d" attempt);
            `String (Response.body_text res))
      in
      `Assoc [ ("name", name); ("greeting", greeting) ])
]}

The function runs in a fiber and may call {!Workero.Promise.await}. Its result
is the output of the instance, an {!Workero.Rpc.value}. An exception that it
raises fails the instance. The runtime creates a new instance of the class for
each run.

[workero build] runs the program once in [workerd] and exports every registered
class from [index.js], like Durable Object and entrypoint classes. It writes
[workflow.js] only when a Workflow is registered, so a Worker without Workflows
imports nothing new. Declare the class in [wrangler.toml]. [class_name] is the
name given to {!Workero.Workflow.register}, and [binding] is the name that
{!Workero.Workflow.of_env} reads:

{@toml[
[[workflows]]
name = "greet"
binding = "GREET"
class_name = "Greet"
]}

{1 Replay}

The runtime runs the function again after every sleep, wait, retry and
eviction. {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_} returns the stored result of a step that
already finished and does not call its callback. So:

- Code outside the steps runs again each time. It must be deterministic: do not
  read the clock or random numbers there. Produce the value inside a step and
  return it, as in the example.
- OCaml values do not live between runs. Keep what later steps need in the
  results of earlier steps.
- A step is identified by its name and the number of calls with that name. Do
  not rename or reorder the steps of instances that already run.
- A step callback runs at least once. Make it idempotent.

{1 Steps}

- {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_}: run a callback and store its
  {!Workero.Rpc.value} result. The callback gets a
  {!Workero.Workflow.Step.type-context} with the [attempt] number: 1 on the
  first try, 2 on the first retry. {!Workero.Workflow.Step.val-retries} sets
  [limit] (0 to 10000), [delay] and [backoff] ([Constant], [Linear] or
  [Exponential]). [timeout] applies to each attempt. Without them the runtime
  uses 5 retries, a 10 second delay, exponential backoff and a 10 minute
  timeout. A step name has at most 256 characters.
  The callback also gets the step name, the step count (2 for the second step
  with that name) and the retry configuration.
  {!Workero.Workflow.Step.retries_with} computes each retry delay from the
  failed attempt. [~rollback] takes a {!Workero.Workflow.Step.val-rollback}
  handler that undoes the step: the runtime calls the handlers in reverse order
  when the instance fails or when
  {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.terminate} has [~rollback:true].
- {!Workero.Workflow.Step.sleep} and {!Workero.Workflow.Step.sleep_until}: wait
  for a {!Workero.Workflow.Duration.t} or until a time in milliseconds since the
  epoch, like {!Workero.Date.now}.
- {!Workero.Workflow.Step.wait_for_event}: wait for an event of a given
  [type_], sent with {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.send_event}, and return the
  event: its type, payload and timestamp. The type has 1 to 100 letters, digits, [-] or [_]. It fails with
  {!Workero.Js_error} after the timeout (24 hours by default). Catch the error
  to go on.

Build a {!Workero.Workflow.Duration.t} with {!Workero.Workflow.Duration.ms},
{!Workero.Workflow.Duration.seconds}, {!Workero.Workflow.Duration.minutes},
{!Workero.Workflow.Duration.hours} or {!Workero.Workflow.Duration.days}. A
negative count raises [Invalid_argument].

A callback that raises fails the attempt, and the runtime retries it. Raise
{!Workero.Workflow.Non_retryable} to fail without retries; the runtime sees a
[NonRetryableError]. After the last attempt, {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_} raises
{!Workero.Js_error} in the function; catch it to keep the instance running.

Step results, event payloads, params and outputs are {!Workero.Rpc.value}s: the
values that [structuredClone] copies. They hold JSON values and also
[`Undefined], [`Bytes], [`Date], [`Bigint], [`Map] and [`Set]. A value
converts to a {!Workero.Rpc.t} with [(v :> Rpc.t)], and
{!Workero.Rpc.to_value} converts back.
A number above 1073741823, such as [Date.now ()], is a [`Float]. A step result
or payload that has no [Rpc.value] form raises [Invalid_argument].

{1 Start and control instances}

Read the binding with {!Workero.Workflow.of_env}:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let handler _req env _ctx =
  let greet = Workflow.of_env env "GREET" in
  let instance =
    Workflow.create greet ~params:(`Assoc [ ("name", `String "Ana") ])
  in
  let state = Workflow.Instance.status instance in
  Response.text
    (Workflow.Instance.id instance
    ^ (match state.status with
      | Workflow.Instance.Complete -> " complete"
      | _ -> " running"))

let () = serve handler
]}

The response body is the instance id followed by [ running]. The first sleep
of the instance lasts 10 seconds, so it has not completed.

{!Workero.Workflow.create} takes an optional [id] and [params]. The params are
the [payload] of the event. {!Workero.Workflow.create_batch} takes a list of
{!Workero.Workflow.val-options}, starts up to 100 instances, and returns the
instances and the errors, for example for an [id] in use.
{!Workero.Workflow.get} finds an instance by id.
{!Workero.Workflow.create_batch_count} starts 1 to 100 instances with the same
params and generated ids, and {!Workero.Workflow.delete_batch} deletes up to 100
instances by id. {!Workero.Workflow.create}, {!Workero.Workflow.val-options} and
{!Workero.Workflow.create_batch_count} take a {!Workero.Workflow.val-retention}
that sets how long the runtime keeps a finished instance.

The {!Workero.Workflow.type-event} of the function also has the
[workflow_name] and the cron [schedule] of an instance that a [schedules] entry
of the binding started.

An instance has {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.id},
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.val-status}, {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.pause},
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.resume}, {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.terminate},
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.restart}, {!Workero.Workflow.Instance.delete} and
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.send_event}. [restart ~from] keeps the stored
results of the steps before the one named by
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.val-restart_from}. The calls suspend like
{!Workero.Promise.await}. They raise {!Workero.Js_error} when the runtime
rejects them, for example for a missing instance, an [id] in use, or a
[send_event] to an instance that is not running. {!Workero.Workflow.create},
{!Workero.Workflow.val-options} and [send_event] raise [Invalid_argument] for an
[id] over 100 characters or an invalid event type.

{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.val-status} returns a
{!Workero.Workflow.Instance.state}: the [status], the [error] as a name and a
message, and the [output] once the instance completes.

To use a Workflow of another Worker, add [script_name] to the
[[[workflows]]] entry in the [wrangler.toml] of the caller.

{1 Limits}

The runtime enforces its
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/reference/limits/}limits}: 1,024
steps (free) or 10,000 (paid, up to 25,000) per instance, 1 MiB for a step
result and for an event payload, 365 days for a sleep, and an instance id of up
to 100 characters. See {{!page-known_issues}Troubleshooting and support} for
what workero leaves out and for what its tests prove.
